SUPERSTORE RETAIL OPERATIONS & SUPPLY CHAIN PLAYBOOK
Document ID: SOP-2026-SUPERSTORE
Department: Strategy, Data Analytics & Operations
Effective Date: January 2026

1. Overview & Dataset Context
This document outlines standard operating procedures for handling demand volatility, inventory optimization, and market basket merchandising across our three primary verticals: Furniture, Office Supplies, and Technology. All sales strategies must align with historical metrics tracking Order Date, Ship Mode, Customer Segment, and Category sales performance.

2. Protocol: Handling Positive Anomalies (Sales Spikes)
A positive anomaly indicates an unexpected surge in Sales volume beyond the LightGBM forecasted baseline. While beneficial, it introduces severe stockout risks, particularly for high-value Technology and Furniture items.

Technology (Phones & Machines): Spikes in this sub-category are often driven by corporate (Corporate Segment) hardware upgrades or back-to-school rushes.

Action: Expedite open purchase orders immediately. Limit bulk purchases by single Customer IDs to prevent inventory hoarding by resellers.

Office Supplies (Binders, Paper, Storage): Spikes here are usually seasonal (e.g., end-of-financial-year reporting).

Action: Since these items have low lead times and are easy to manufacture, engage secondary local suppliers immediately to fulfill Standard Class shipments without upgrading to First Class.

Furniture (Tables, Chairs, Bookcases): Spikes in furniture can cause massive logistical bottlenecks due to size.

Action: Pause paid advertising on trending Product Names. Ensure warehouse staging areas are cleared to handle incoming freight.

3. Protocol: Handling Negative Anomalies (Sales Drops)
A negative anomaly indicates an unexpected drop in demand, leading to excess inventory and tied-up working capital.

Root Cause Analysis (RCA):

Geographic Drops: Filter data by Region (East, West, South, Central) and State. Determine if the drop is localized due to weather events or regional economic shifts.

Shipping Issues: Analyze Ship Mode distributions. If Same Day or First Class metrics drop, investigate carrier delays causing customer churn.

Inventory Clearance Strategies:

Bundling: Bundle slow-moving Office Supplies (e.g., Labels, Envelopes) with high-performing Technology products at a slight discount.

Segment Targeting: If Consumer segment sales drop, shift marketing focus to B2B by offering bulk discounts to the Home Office and Corporate segments.

4. Category-Specific Sales Improvement Strategies
To continuously improve baseline sales and Average Order Value (AOV), implement the following category-specific logic:

Furniture: Sales often suffer from high shipping costs and long delivery times. Strategy: Offer free Standard Class shipping thresholds (e.g., "Free shipping on Bookcases over $500"). Create digital showrooms pairing Chairs with Tables to encourage set purchases.

Technology: These are high-margin, high-consideration purchases. Strategy: Cross-sell low-margin, high-volume accessories. Never sell a machine or phone without pitching an extended warranty or matching Accessories.

Office Supplies: This category relies on repeat volume. Strategy: Implement subscription models for consumable Sub-Categories like Paper, Binders, and Art supplies targeted at the Corporate segment to guarantee recurring revenue.

5. Market Basket Analysis (MBA) & Cross-Merchandising
To increase sales, products must be strategically paired based on association rule mining (Support, Confidence, and Lift).

The "Desk Setup" Basket: Pair Furniture (Desks/Tables) with Technology (Monitors/Accessories) and Office Supplies (Storage).

Loss Leader Merchandising: If Fasteners or Labels are heavily discounted to drive traffic, ensure the checkout path forces exposure to high-margin goods like Appliances or Copiers.